PROJECT G.A.L.A.C.T.I.C. — Simulation‑Ready Spec

Architect: C. Matthew BlackwellTarget: Numerical gravitational–antigravity anomaly simulation (Python‑friendly, engine‑ready).

1. Simulation requirements

Language: Python (or similar) with:

NumPy (arrays, linear algebra)

SciPy (ODE / integrators) or custom symplectic integrator

Optional: Matplotlib for visualization

Core capabilities:

Integrate Hamiltonian dynamics over time

Compute pairwise gravitational interactions

Apply antigravity/coupling via parameters

Track structural stress and classify regimes

2. Data model

Particles / nodes:

Position:[ r_i \in \mathbb{R}^3 ]

Momentum:[ p_i \in \mathbb{R}^3 ]

Mass: (m_i \in \mathbb{R}^+)

Global parameters:

Gravitational constant: (G)

Cosmological term: (\Lambda)

Coupling constants: (\alpha, \beta, \gamma)

Time step: (\Delta t), total time: (T)

Tensegrity structure:

Edges: list of ((i, j)) pairs

Rest lengths: (L_{ij})

Stiffness / tension coefficients: (k_{ij})

3. Core equations

3.1 Hamiltonian

[ H = \sum_i \left( \frac{p_i^2}{2m_i} + V(r_i) \right)

\sum_{i < j} \frac{G m_i m_j}{r_{ij}}

\Lambda \sum_i r_i^2 ]

Where:

[ r_{ij} = |r_i - r_j| ]

3.2 Evolution (Hamilton’s equations)

[ \dot{r}_i = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i} = \frac{p_i}{m_i} ]

[ \dot{p}_i = -\frac{\partial H}{\partial r_i} ]

Numerically integrated with a symplectic integrator (e.g. leapfrog):

Position update: [ r_i^{(n+1)} = r_i^{(n)} + \Delta t \cdot \frac{p_i^{(n+1/2)}}{m_i} ]

Momentum half‑step: [ p_i^{(n+1/2)} = p_i^{(n)} + \frac{\Delta t}{2} F_i^{(n)} ]

Momentum full‑step: [ p_i^{(n+1)} = p_i^{(n+1/2)} + \frac{\Delta t}{2} F_i^{(n+1)} ]

Where (F_i) is derived from gravitational + antigravity + potential forces.

4. Force model (including antigravity)

Gravitational pair force (i on j):

[ F_{ij}^{\text{grav}} = - G \frac{m_i m_j}{r_{ij}^3} (r_i - r_j) ]

Antigravity / coupling term:

Implement as a tunable modification to effective (G), or an additional radial term: [ F_i^{\Lambda} = -2 \Lambda r_i ]

Or use (\beta, \gamma) to scale curvature‑like or oscillatory contributions: [ F_i^{\text{couple}} = f(\beta, \gamma, r_i, p_i) ]

Total force:

[ F_i = \sum_{j \neq i} F_{ij}^{\text{grav}} + F_i^{\Lambda} + F_i^{\text{couple}} ]

5. Tensegrity & stress computation

Edge extension: [ \Delta L_{ij} = r_{ij} - L_{ij} ]

Edge stress: [ \sigma_{ij} = k_{ij} \cdot \Delta L_{ij} ]

Global stress functional: [ S_{\text{geom}} = \sum_{(i,j)} \sigma_{ij}^2 ]

Stress trend: track (S_{\text{geom}}(t)) over time.

6. Regime classification logic

At each time window (e.g. every (N) steps):

Compute:

Average acceleration magnitude:[ \bar{a}(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i |\dot{p}_i(t)/m_i| ]

Stress level: (S_{\text{geom}}(t))

Stress derivative: (\frac{dS_{\text{geom}}}{dt})

Classify:

Warped: high (S_{\text{geom}}) and (|dS/dt|) above threshold

Stable: bounded (S_{\text{geom}}), small (|dS/dt|)

Accelerating: monotonic increase in (\bar{a}(t)) or (S_{\text{geom}}(t))

Store regime labels in a timeline array.

7. Simulation workflow

Initialize:

Set (r_i(0), p_i(0), m_i) from chosen scenario or astrophysical proxy.

Define edges, (L_{ij}), (k_{ij}).

Set (\Lambda, \alpha, \beta, \gamma), (T), (\Delta t).

Loop over time steps:

Compute forces (F_i).

Integrate positions and momenta via symplectic scheme.

Compute tensegrity stress (S_{\text{geom}}).

Periodically classify regime and log metrics.

Output:

Trajectories (r_i(t), p_i(t)).

Stress time series (S_{\text{geom}}(t)).

Regime timeline (warped / stable / accelerating).

Parameter set used for the run.

